# Rebuild to verify

After editing compilable code (C++/CMake/headers, Qt UI wiring, etc.), **rebuild the project before finishing the turn**. IDE/linter diagnostics help but **do not** replace a real compile for this codebase (typedefs vs forward declares, template instantiation, link errors, `-Werror`-class issues).

## Hard gate (do not skip)

- A turn that changed compilable files is **not done** until a build of the affected target(s) has been **run in this turn** and exited **0**.
- Saying “should compile”, relying on an earlier session’s build, or citing only ReadLints / IDE diagnostics **does not** satisfy this rule.
- If the build fails: fix and rebuild in the **same turn** until clean. Do not hand broken trees back to the user.
- In the final reply, briefly state what was built and that it succeeded (or that the user asked to skip).

## Required bar

- Incremental or full build of the affected targets succeeds.
- **No new compiler errors.**
- **No new compiler warnings** introduced by the change (fix or avoid them in the same turn).
- If new sources were added under a CMake-listed or reconfigure-sensitive tree, **reconfigure** when needed, then build.

## How

- Prefer a quiet build so logs stay short for diagnosis:  
  `cmake --build build --target <target> -j$(sysctl -n hw.ncpu) -- -s`  
  (or `make -sj…` in `build/`). `QUIET_BUILD` (default ON) already hides per-file progress; `-s` hides recipe echo. Compiler errors/warnings still appear.
- Use `macos/build.sh` / `macos/build-using-homebrew.sh` when a full/configure cycle is needed.
- Scope to what changed when reliable (`dcpp`, `eiskaltdcpp-qt`, …); widen if public headers or cross-lib APIs moved.
- After adding/moving `dcpp/` sources, reconfigure when CMake lists change.
- Headers under `dcpp/<subdir>/` must include dcpp roots as `"../Foo.h"` (same pattern as `hub/`, `queue/`) so Qt translation units that pull them via `dcpp/HashManager.h` still resolve.
- On failure: fix and rebuild in the same turn until clean. Do not paste full successful build logs into the reply.

## Skip only when

- The edit is docs/rules/assets/non-compiled text with no build impact, **or**
- The user explicitly says not to build.

Do **not** skip because a linter reported no issues.
